## Step 3: Tier 1 unit tests for existing Services and Models

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#57

### Summary
Add pure, DB-free unit tests for the app's existing `Services/` and validation logic in
`Models/`/`Common`.

### Motivation
`Services/` and `Common/ModelConstants` contain the small amount of pure logic in the app.
These are the cheapest tests to write and maintain and should exist before the heavier
controller tier is built out.

### Proposed Changes
- `Services/UniqueCodeGenerator.cs` — `GenerateUniqueCodeAsync` and `MaxAttempts` changed from
  `private` to `internal`, plus `[InternalsVisibleTo("WhenWorksWeb.Tests")]` on
  `WhenWorksWeb.csproj`. This gives tests a deterministic seam onto the retry/max-attempts loop
  via a stub existence-check delegate, independent of the actual (non-seedable, cryptographic)
  RNG behind code generation — needed to test collision-retry and exhaustion behavior at all,
  since a real collision can't be forced from outside.
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Services/UniqueCodeGeneratorTests.cs` — code length/alphabet correctness,
  retry-until-success, exact-attempt-count exhaustion (pinned to `UniqueCodeGenerator.MaxAttempts`
  rather than a duplicated magic number), exception propagation from `existsAsync` (not
  swallowed/wrapped), and an RNG sanity check (many independent generations aren't all identical).
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Common/ModelConstantsTests.cs` — narrowed to facts about the constants
  themselves (alphabet excludes ambiguous characters, no duplicate characters, alphabet/pattern
  character-class coherence, default color/emoji length bounds). Does not re-test validation
  behavior — see below for why that moved to real model tests.
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Models/IndexViewModelTests.cs` and `ParticipantTests.cs` (plus
  `EventMessageTests.cs`/`ApplicationUserTests.cs` for parity) — test `EventCode`/`Color`
  validation via `System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations.Validator.TryValidateProperty` against
  the real model classes, exercising the actual `[Required]`/`[StringLength]`/
  `[RegularExpression]` attributes exactly as ASP.NET Core model binding would, rather than
  reimplementing an attribute's match semantics by hand in test code (which was the initial
  approach, then replaced — see below).
- **Real gap found and fixed during this step**: `Participant.Color`, `EventMessage.SenderColor`,
  and `ApplicationUser.Color` each pair `[RegularExpression(HexColorPattern)]` with
  `[StringLength(HexColorLength)]` but no `MinimumLength` and no `[Required]`. Since
  `RegularExpressionAttribute` treats null/empty as valid by design (deferring emptiness
  enforcement to `[Required]`/`MinimumLength`), an empty string passed both attributes —
  discovered when a model-level test (not the earlier pattern-only test) caught it. Not
  reachable via any live code path today (the sole entry point, `EventSignInViewModel.Color`,
  already has `[Required]`), but a latent gap for any future direct write to these properties.
  Fixed by adding `MinimumLength = HexColorLength` to all three `[StringLength]` attributes. See
  `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`'s Domain Conventions & Gotchas for the documented version.

### Acceptance Criteria
- All Tier 1 tests run with no database, HTTP context, or controller involved.
- `dotnet test` passes for the new test files (62 tests as of this step).
- DataAnnotations-validated properties are tested via `Validator.TryValidateProperty` against the
  real model class, not a hand-rolled reimplementation of attribute match semantics.

### Out of Scope
- Any controller or HTTP-pipeline test (Steps 5-7).

### Dependencies
Step 1 (test project must exist).

