## Step 6: WebApplicationFactory harness and initial Tier 3 smoke tests

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#60

### Summary
Build a full-pipeline test harness and a short list of smoke tests for behaviors only the
real HTTP pipeline exercises.

### Motivation
Authentication middleware, routing, and anti-forgery filters aren't exercised by calling
controller actions directly. This tier is deliberately small — a handful of smoke tests, not
broad coverage.

### Proposed Changes
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Fixtures/CustomWebApplicationFactory.cs` — a `WebApplicationFactory<Program>`
  that swaps `ApplicationDbContext` from SQL Server to a real SQLite `:memory:` database (same
  collation-registration requirement as `SqliteDbContextFixture`, applied via DI service
  replacement in `ConfigureWebHost` instead of direct construction).
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Fixtures/AntiForgeryTokenExtractor.cs` — pulls the real
  `__RequestVerificationToken` value out of rendered form HTML so smoke tests submit POSTs
  through the actual antiforgery pipeline rather than bypassing it.
- Smoke test: full create event -> join event -> sign in flow via `HttpClient` (two separate
  clients/cookie containers, simulating two different browsers), with a final check against the
  database (not just the rendered page) confirming the participant was actually persisted.
- Smoke test: an anonymous request to an `Areas/Admin` route is rejected (redirected to the
  Identity login page, not served or 500'd).
- Smoke test: a state-changing POST without a valid antiforgery token is rejected (400), with a
  database check confirming nothing was persisted.
- **Two real blockers found and fixed, both verified rather than guessed at:**
  - Removing only the `DbContextOptions<ApplicationDbContext>` descriptor before re-adding a
    SQLite-configured one was not sufficient — EF Core 8+'s `AddDbContext` registers each
    configuration call as a separate, additive `IDbContextOptionsConfiguration<ApplicationDbContext>`
    entry, so Program.cs's original `UseSqlServer` call stayed composed in and both providers ended
    up registered simultaneously ("Services for database providers ... have been registered").
    Fixed by also removing `IDbContextOptionsConfiguration<ApplicationDbContext>` before re-adding.
  - The full-flow smoke test initially failed with an inexplicable redirect back to the sign-in
    page after a sign-in POST that had actually succeeded (confirmed via diagnostic output that the
    participant was already persisted). Root cause: `SetEventAccessCookie` correctly sets
    `Secure = true`, but the default test client's `http://localhost` base address made
    `CookieContainer` silently refuse to resend that cookie on the following request. Fixed by
    giving every smoke-test client an `https://` base address (TestServer never opens a real
    socket either way — this only changes how `CookieContainer` treats the `Secure` flag).

### Acceptance Criteria
- All three smoke tests pass against the real ASP.NET Core pipeline (routing, middleware,
  Identity all engaged, not bypassed).
- The factory correctly isolates its database from any other test run.

### Out of Scope
- Broad Tier 3 coverage beyond the three smoke tests listed — this tier stays intentionally
  small (see file intro).

### Dependencies
Step 1. Reuses entity builders from Step 4 where practical. Blocks Step 7's
authorization/anti-forgery tests.

---

## Step 7: Security-focused test coverage

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#61

### Summary
Add explicit, deliberate coverage of authorization boundaries, anti-forgery enforcement, and
input validation boundaries.

### Motivation
Security was flagged as a top priority for this test system. This step makes sure these
behaviors are explicitly covered, rather than only incidentally covered as a side effect of
the feature-focused tests in Steps 5 and 6.

### Proposed Changes
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Fixtures/TestAuthHandler.cs` — a test-only authentication scheme
  (registered in `CustomWebApplicationFactory`) that authenticates a request as a given user
  id/role set from `X-Test-UserId`/`X-Test-Roles` headers, standing in for Identity's real
  login/cookie mechanics while leaving `[Authorize]` policy evaluation itself completely real.
  `DefaultChallengeScheme`/`DefaultForbidScheme` stay pointed at Identity's actual cookie scheme
  so unauthenticated/unauthorized redirects still match production exactly.
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Security/AdminAuthorizationTests.cs` — `[Authorize(Roles="Admin")]`
  boundary on `Areas/Admin/Pages/Users/ManageUsers.cshtml.cs`: anonymous (redirect to login),
  authenticated without the Admin role (redirect to access-denied), authenticated with a
  *different* role (still redirected — proves the check is role-specific, not "any authenticated
  role"), and authenticated with the Admin role (200 OK) — both the closed and open cases.
- `WhenWorksWeb.Tests/Security/AntiForgeryEnforcementTests.cs` — missing-token rejection for
  `Events/Join`, `event/{code}/signin`, and `MyEvents/Delete` (the latter specifically while
  *authenticated*, isolating antiforgery enforcement from the `[Authorize]` check so one failure
  can't mask the other), each with a database check confirming nothing was persisted.
  `EventsController.Create` was already covered by Step 6's general smoke test.
- `EventsControllerSignInTests.Post_ExistingParticipant_WithAnotherParticipantsRejoinCode_AddsModelError`
  — a rejoin code valid for a *different* participant in the same event must not grant access to
  the selected one; proves the code is checked against the specific selected participant, not
  "any valid code in this event." Builds on Step 5's existing missing/incorrect/correct rejoin
  code tests rather than duplicating them.
- `ModelConstants`-driven boundary tests added for properties Step 3/5 hadn't directly covered:
  `Participant.DisplayName`, `ApplicationUser.DisplayName`, `Event.Code`/`Title`, and
  `EventSignInViewModel.DisplayName`/`Color`/`RejoinCode` (the last is the actual form-bound
  model behind the sign-in POST, so its boundaries are the most directly security-relevant in
  the app) — each via `Validator.TryValidateProperty` against the real model, per the Step 3
  convention.
- **Real gap found and fixed**: `ApplicationUser.DisplayName` had the identical
  `[StringLength(MaxLength)]`-with-no-`MinimumLength` shape as the `Color` gap fixed in Step 3 —
  an empty display name silently passed validation. Fixed by adding `MinimumLength = 1`. See
  `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`'s Domain Conventions & Gotchas, now generalized to cover both the
  `[StringLength]`-alone and `[StringLength]`+`[RegularExpression]` variants of this gap.
- **One case deliberately not "fixed"**: `Event.Code` has no `[Required]` and a bare
  `Validator.TryValidateProperty(null, ...)` call against it passes — but `Code` is `required`
  (compile-time enforced) and only ever set via `Event.Create` with an already-validated,
  already-generated code, never form-bound. There's no reachable path for a null `Code`, so
  unlike the `Color`/`DisplayName` gaps this isn't something to fix — noted in
  `EventTests.cs` rather than silently omitted.

### Acceptance Criteria
- Every `[Authorize]`-gated route has a test confirming unauthorized/unauthenticated
  requests are rejected.
- Every state-changing POST action has a test confirming a missing/invalid anti-forgery
  token is rejected.
- `ModelConstants` boundary tests cover both the valid-boundary and just-past-boundary cases
  for each constrained field.
- `dotnet test` passes for the full suite (142 tests as of this step).

### Out of Scope
- General business-logic correctness already covered in Step 5.

### Dependencies
Step 4 (SQLite harness, for validation-level tests) and Step 6 (WebApplicationFactory
harness, for authorization/anti-forgery tests requiring the real HTTP pipeline).

